<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="CursorAI.App" />

  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <!--
    CursorAI's capture path treats GetCursorPos, GetMonitorInfo and BitBlt results as true
    physical pixels. That only holds for a per-monitor-DPI-aware process.

    Measured 2026-10-08 on Windows 11 26200, .NET 10 WPF, single 1920x1080 display at 120 DPI:
      without this manifest : PROCESS_SYSTEM_DPI_AWARE  (awareness 1)
      with this manifest    : PROCESS_PER_MONITOR_AWARE (awareness 2)

    System-aware happens to be correct on a single display but virtualises coordinates on a
    second monitor at a different scale factor, so this declaration is what makes the capture
    path sound. Declared explicitly so the requirement comes from the project rather than from
    a framework default that can change between .NET versions.

    KNOWN DISCREPANCY: we request PerMonitorV2 but the process resolves to per-monitor V1
    (awareness 2, not 3). Removing the legacy dpiAware element above does not change this, so
    the downgrade comes from the WPF host, not from this file. V1 satisfies the only property
    CursorAI depends on - unvirtualised physical coordinates - because V2 adds non-client-area
    scaling, dialog auto-scaling and child-window DPI notifications, none of which apply to a
    borderless 64x64 window that positions itself. Revisit if CursorAI ever grows real chrome.

    STILL UNVERIFIED: multi-monitor and mixed-DPI behaviour. The development machine has one
    display, so CursorGrounding's negative-origin math has never actually executed.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/PM</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
